      "text": "To determine the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\( f(x) = (3x+4)(7x+1)(13x+6)(2x+9) \\) and \\( x \\), we start by considering the factors in \\( f(x) \\).\n\nFirst, note that if \\( x \\) is a multiple of 15336, the GCD of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\) is the product of the common factors between \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\). We need to find such common factors.\n\n### Step 1: Identify Common Factors\nConsider the factors of 15336:\n\\[\n15336 = 2^9 \\times 3^2 \\times 7\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Analyze Each Term in \\( f(x) \\)\nFor \\( f(x) \\) to be divisible by \\( x \\), each term in \\( f(x) \\) must have at least one factor of \\( x \\):\n- \\( 3x+4 \\)\n- \\( 7x+1 \\)\n- \\( 13x+6 \\)\n- \\( 2x+9 \\)\n\n### Step 3: Check for Common Divisors\nFor \\( x \\) to divide \\( f(x) \\), each term must have at least one factor of \\( x \\).\n\nTo determine the GCD, we check if there are common factors:\n- \\( 3x+4 \\) implies \\( 3x \\) or \\( 2 \\) (by division by 3).\n\n- \\( 7x+1 \\) implies \\( 7x \\) or \\( 1 \\) (by division by 7).\n\n- \\( 13x+6 \\) implies \\( 13x \\) or \\( 6 \\) (by division by 13).\n  \n- \\( 2x+9 \\) implies \\( 2x \\) or \\( 9 \\) (by division by 2).\n\n### Step 4: Simplify by Considering \\( x = k \\) (not necessarily a multiple of 15336)\nSince \\( x \\) can be any multiple of 15336, consider \\( x = 15336 \\):\n\nWe check the GCD components:\n\n- \\( x = 15336 \\):\n  \\[\n  3x+4 = 3(15336) + 4 = 46008 + 4 = 46012\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  7x+1 = 7(15336) + 1 = 107352 + 1 = 107353\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  13x+6 = 13(15336) + 6 = 200088 + 6 = 200094\n  \\]\n  \\[\n  2x+9 = 2(15336) + 9 = 30672 + 9 = 30681\n  \\]\n\n### Step 5: Find Lowest Common Divisor\nThe least common multiple (LCM) of all these terms (excluding \\( x \\)) does not depend on \\( x \\):\n- The LCM of \\(46012, 107353, 200094, 30681\\) is the LCM of these numbers.\n\nTo find the GCD, calculate the LCM:\nCalculate the GCD of 46012 and 107353:\n\n1. Define:\n   \\[\n   a = 46012 = 2^2 \\times 11503\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b = 107353 = 11 \\times 9759\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD}(11, 9759) = 11\n   \\]\n   Hence,\n   \\[\n   GCD(a, b) = 11\n   \\]\n\nCheck other LCMs with 9759:\n\\[\n\\text{CVG}(k) = (\\text{LCM}(k), 11) \\]\nResulting from 11 common factors:\n\nThe final GCD of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{3616}\n\\]",
