      "text": "To solve for the greatest common divisor (GCD) of \\( f(x) = (3x+4)(7x+1)(13x+6)(2x+9) \\) and \\( x = 15336 \\), we can start by analyzing the structure of the polynomial \\( f(x) \\) and using the properties of GCD. We will use the fact that if \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are integers, then \\(\\gcd(a, b \\times n) = \\gcd(a, n)\\) for any integer \\( n \\), and we can use the Euclidean algorithm to simplify the problem.\n\nFirst, observe that \\( x = 15336 \\) is divisible by several smaller numbers. We will calculate the prime factorization of \\( x \\):\n\n1. Calculate 3:\n\\[ 3 \\mid 15336 \\quad \\text{(24448 + 2 = 15336)} \\]\n2. Then, calculate 7:\n\\[ 7 \\mid 13055 \\quad \\text{(15499 + 41 = 15336)} \\]\n3. Then, calculate 13:\n\\[ 13 \\mid 535 \\quad \\text{(558 + 11 = 535)} \\]\n4. Finally, calculate 2:\n\\[ 2 \\mid 9 \\quad \\text{(6 = 24 - 18; 42 = 48 - 6)} \\]\n\nThrough the calculation, the Prime factorization of 15336 is:\n\\[ 15336 = 2^4 \\times 3^1 \\times 7^1 \\times 13^1 \\]\n\\[ x = 15336 = 2^4 \\times 3 \\times 7 \\times 13 \\]\n\nLet \\[ f(x) = (3x+4)(7x+1)(13x+6)(2x+9) \\]\nIdentify common primes:\n1. 2 \\[ (3 \\times 15336 + 4) = (46008 + 4 = 46012) \\] is divisible by 2 \\[ (7 \\times 15336 + 1) = (107349 + 1 = 107350) \\] is divisible by 2 \n2. 2^2 \\[ (13 \\times 15336 + 6) = (200312 + 6 = 200318) \\] is divisible by 2\n3. 3 \\[ (2 \\times 15336 + 9) = (30672 + 9 = 30681) \\] is divisible by 3\n\nAdditionally:\n1. 5 \\[ (1536 + 1) = (1537) \\] leaves remainder 1\n2. 7 \\[ (1536 + 1) = (1537) \\] leaves remainder 6\n2. 13 \\[ (1536 + 1) = (1537) \\] leaves remainder 6\n\nNow, we know that 2^3 (2^4 × 3, or \\( 3 \\times 15336 \\)) is divisible by 8. But we are missing the prime factor 5. Further, the product 17 was obtained with a remainder of 1 for every factor. Hence, the GCD does not involve prime factor 5 and not all prime factors of \\( x \\). It is:\n\\[ \\gcd \\left( f(x), x \\right) = f(1) = 2^4 \\times 3 \\times 13 \\]\nSo the GCD of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( x \\) is:\n\n[ \\boxed{1536} ]",
